{"y":1995,"label":"1994–95","coach":"Jim Calhoun","conf":"Big East","confShort":"Big East","w":28,"l":5,"cw":16,"cl":2,"confFinish":"1st in Big East","finish":"elite8","seed":2,"region":"West","apPre":19,"apHigh":1,"apFinal":8,"srs":19.23,"sos":7.65,"ortg":107.4,"drtg":92.9,"ppg":86.0,"oppg":74.4,"story":{"headline":"Ray Allen Takes Flight","text":"Sophomore Ray Allen became a star — 21.1 points per game and first-team All-Big East — and UConn became a juggernaut. The Huskies started 15–0, a run that included a 90–86 win over No. 6 Duke, climbed to No. 1, and went 16–2 to repeat as Big East regular-season champions, sweeping Syracuse along the way. In the Big East tournament UConn beat Pittsburgh and Georgetown before Villanova won the final 94–78. As a No. 2 seed in the West, the Huskies beat Chattanooga, Cincinnati and Maryland — senior Donny Marshall averaged more than 24 points in the tournament — before falling to top-seeded UCLA 102–96 in the Elite Eight. Guard Kevin Ollie, a future UConn head coach, made third-team All-Big East.","moments":[{"date":"1995-03-12","title":"Big East tournament Championship: L vs Villanova","text":"UConn lost to Villanova 78–94 (Championship) at Madison Square Garden, New York."},{"date":"1995-03-16","title":"NCAA First Round: W vs Chattanooga","text":"UConn beat Chattanooga 100–71 in the NCAA tournament first round at Jon M. Huntsman Center, Salt Lake City."},{"date":"1995-03-18","title":"NCAA Second Round: W vs Cincinnati","text":"UConn beat Cincinnati 96–91 in the NCAA tournament second round at Jon M. Huntsman Center, Salt Lake City."},{"date":"1995-03-23","title":"NCAA Sweet Sixteen: W vs Maryland","text":"UConn beat Maryland 99–89 in the NCAA tournament sweet sixteen at Oakland–Alameda County Coliseum Arena, Oakland, California."},{"date":"1995-03-25","title":"NCAA Elite Eight: L vs UCLA","text":"UConn lost to UCLA 96–102 in the NCAA tournament elite eight at Oakland–Alameda County Coliseum Arena, Oakland, California."}],"ho